The Security Council,

Recalling all of its previous resolutions, statements of its President, and press statements on the situation in the Central African Republic (CAR), in particular resolutions 2659 (2022), 2693 (2023), 2745 (2024), and 2759 (2024),

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity, and territorial integrity of the CAR, and recalling the importance of the principles of non-interference, good-neighbourliness, and regional cooperation,

Reaffirming the basic principles of peacekeeping, including consent of the parties, impartiality, and non-use of force, except in self-defence and defence of the mandate, and recalling in this regard resolution 2436 (2018),

Recalling that the CAR Government has the primary responsibility to protect all populations in the CAR, recognizing the persistent security challenges threatening civilians, and commending the continued efforts and sacrifices of the Central African Armed Forces (FACA) and the United Nations Multidimensional Integrated Stabilization Mission in the Central African Republic (MINUSCA) to restore State authority and maintain stability,

Reiterating that the 2019 Political Agreement for Peace and Reconciliation in the CAR (APPR) and the Luanda Roadmap adopted under the auspices of the International Conference on the Great Lakes Region (ICGLR) remain the central framework for achieving lasting peace and stability, welcoming the dissolution and disarming of several armed groups, and urging all remaining armed groups to immediately cease hostilities, lay down their weapons, and engage constructively in the peace process without preconditions,

Condemning in the strongest terms all attacks, violations of international humanitarian law, and human rights violations and abuses committed by armed groups and other actors, including killings, sexual and gender-based violence, recruitment and use of children, attacks against schools and medical facilities, and the illicit exploitation of natural resources,

Expressing grave concern over the increasing and indiscriminate use of explosive ordnance, including landmines and improvised explosive devices, by armed groups, which endangers civilians, peacekeepers, humanitarian personnel, and national security forces, and calling for enhanced mine-action capabilities and international support to address this threat,

Expressing deep concern about the spillover effects of regional conflicts, in particular the ongoing crisis in the Sudan, on the security, humanitarian, and socio-economic situation in the CAR, particularly in border regions, and welcoming the CAR Government’s generosity in hosting refugees and returnees despite severe domestic constraints,

Stressing the imperative of combating impunity and ensuring accountability for all violations and abuses of international human rights law and international humanitarian law, and commending the work of the Special Criminal Court (SCC) and the ordinary courts, as well as the Truth, Justice, Reparation and Reconciliation Commission,

Underlining the importance of the upcoming electoral cycles, notably the local and general elections, as critical milestones to consolidate democracy, decentralization, and national reconciliation, and calling upon the CAR authorities, with the support of MINUSCA and international partners, to ensure their timely, transparent, credible, inclusive, and peaceful conduct, with the full, equal, and meaningful participation of women and youth,

Condemning in the strongest terms all attacks, provocations, and disinformation and misinformation campaigns targeting MINUSCA and its personnel, emphasizing that such attacks may constitute war crimes, and calling upon the CAR Government to take all appropriate steps to bring perpetrators to justice,

Welcoming the dedication of MINUSCA’s military, police, and civilian personnel and paying tribute to peacekeepers who have made the ultimate sacrifice in the service of peace in the CAR,

1. Decides to extend the mandate of MINUSCA until 15 November 2026;

2. Decides to maintain the current troop ceiling of MINUSCA at 14,400 military personnel, 3,020 police personnel, and 108 corrections officers;

3. Authorizes MINUSCA to take all necessary means to carry out its mandate within its capabilities and areas of deployment;

4. Decides that the mandate of MINUSCA shall continue to prioritize the following tasks:
(a) Protection of civilians, through proactive, robust, and dynamic patrolling, active deterrence, and rapid deployment to areas under threat;
(b) Good offices and support to the peace process, including the continuous implementation of the APPR and the Luanda Roadmap, facilitating inclusive national dialogue, and supporting local peace and reconciliation initiatives;
(c) Support for the preparation and holding of peaceful, transparent, inclusive, and credible elections, including by providing technical, logistical, and operational assistance, as well as coordinated security support;
(d) Facilitation of the safe, rapid, unhindered, and sustained delivery of humanitarian assistance across the territory of the CAR;
(e) Promotion and protection of human rights, including monitoring, investigating, and reporting on violations and abuses of human rights and international humanitarian law, and supporting efforts to end impunity;

5. Further decides that MINUSCA shall carry out the following additional tasks:
(a) Support for the extension of State authority, the deployment of civil administration, and the reform of the security sector (SSR), in coordination with international partners;
(b) Support for disarmament, demobilization, reintegration (DDR), and community violence reduction (CVR) programs, as well as repatriation of foreign combatants;
(c) Support for national and international justice, the fight against impunity, and the rule of law, including continued technical and operational support to the Special Criminal Court;
(d) Assistance to the CAR authorities in preventing and countering the illicit proliferation and trafficking of small arms, light weapons, and the illicit exploitation of natural resources;
(e) Support for mine-action activities, explosive ordnance risk education, and the mitigation of threats posed by explosive devices;

6. Reaffirms the importance of MINUSCA’s freedom of movement, unhindered operational capability, and full compliance with the Status of Forces Agreement (SOFA), and urges the CAR authorities to ensure the safety, security, and unimpeded access of MINUSCA personnel throughout the territory;

7. Calls upon the CAR Government and regional partners to intensify cross-border cooperation to enhance border security, stem the illicit flow of arms and fighters, and mitigate the humanitarian and security impacts of the crisis in the Sudan;

8. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Security Council on the situation in the CAR and the implementation of MINUSCA’s mandate by 15 February 2026, 15 June 2026, and 15 October 2026;

9. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

